## 9. Investigate tension and resistance

Do not present the philosophical model as harmonious or complete.

Identify explicit tensions involving:

- AI dependence
- loss of agency
- loss of human identity
- false understanding
- excessive agreement
- over-interpretation
- fluent but incorrect answers
- the finished-sounding answer
- memory becoming authority
- false continuity
- authority drift
- model replacement
- privacy
- human responsibility
- loss of friction
- the relationship becoming an echo chamber
- the right not to be interpreted
- the right to remain unfinished

For each tension, explain:

- whether it is explicitly addressed
- where it appears
- what response is proposed
- whether the response is implemented or philosophical
- what remains unresolved

Pay particular attention to the idea that:

> A healthy relationship contains resistance.

---

## 10. Investigate becoming

Examine what the work suggests may happen over time.

Determine whether “becoming” refers to:

- the human changing
- AI becoming more integrated into human thinking
- the relationship developing
- a project acquiring continuity
- a shared working identity evolving
- the emergence of new creativity
- the growth of an extended mind
- a cultural change in how intelligence is understood
- a future system that does not yet exist

Identify what the work says should remain stable while other things change.

Pay attention to:

- human purpose
- human responsibility
- the human centre
- the distinction between human and AI
- the right to revise
- continuity without sameness
- growth without replacement
- the relationship remaining unfinished

