## 12. Investigate whether the book demonstrates its own thesis

Determine whether the public structure supports the possibility that:

- fragments become concepts
- concepts become chapters
- chapters produce new questions
- the book is a synthesis rather than a starting point
- the repository is part of the thinking process
- the writing process itself demonstrates human-AI interaction
- the book documents the system while also theorizing about it

If supported, explain the evidence.

If this is only a reasonable interpretation, label it:

```text
[INFERRED]
```

Do not claim a formal pipeline unless it is explicitly documented.

---

## 13. Philosophical failure modes

Identify the deepest unresolved philosophical problems.

Look for questions such as:

- Can an AI participate in meaning without experiencing a life?
- Can relational intelligence exist without AI consciousness?
- Is a shared identity possible without shared embodiment?
- Does memory create identity or only the appearance of identity?
- When does extension become dependence?
- When does collaboration become replacement?
- Can the relationship remain ethical if one participant cannot bear
  consequences?
- Can human responsibility remain clear when the process becomes deeply shared?
- Is the third mind a useful concept or an attractive metaphor?
- Can continuity survive model replacement without becoming false continuity?
- What must remain private or unstructured?
- Can uncertainty be preserved when language constantly pushes toward completion?
- What happens when the relationship begins to reproduce its own assumptions?

Distinguish questions explicitly raised by the author from questions introduced
by your analysis.

